1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
It spread the word of God and war.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:13,000
It set us free and spelled out our rights.

3
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:19,000
It tells stories, sells products, and solves crimes.

4
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:23,000
From fine print to big business,

5
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:28,000
this ancient substance has written its way into the very history it was used to write.

6
00:00:28,001 --> 00:00:31,240
Now, Inc. on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:43,060 --> 00:00:47,460
Inc. is one of the most ubiquitous man-made substances on Earth.

8
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:50,160
Just look around.

9
00:00:51,100 --> 00:00:52,380
It's everywhere.

10
00:00:53,880 --> 00:00:56,180
Inc. is one of those things that's around you.

11
00:00:56,181 --> 00:00:58,740
it surrounds you and people don't think about it.

12
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:01,540
For example, if you go into the grocery store, you're surrounded by ink.

13
00:01:02,020 --> 00:01:03,340
You have ink on the money.

14
00:01:04,220 --> 00:01:05,300
You have ink on the packages.

15
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:09,500
You can have ink on the tiles on the floor, plus whatever magazines you might buy.

16
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:13,280
So ink is all around us, but when I say I work for an ink company,

17
00:01:13,880 --> 00:01:19,840
people ask me about pens, or they try to score a computer printer type ink for me.

18
00:01:19,841 --> 00:01:28,340
I think the best way to define an ink is any kind of liquid or paste that carries colorant

19
00:01:28,341 --> 00:01:29,440
in it.

20
00:01:29,441 --> 00:01:34,020
And after the ink dries, it leaves the colorant on the paper.

21
00:01:34,021 --> 00:01:46,320
A close cousin of paint, ink is primarily used for writing and printing.

22
00:01:46,321 --> 00:01:50,920
Ink is made up of a few basic components.

23
00:01:50,921 --> 00:01:58,340
The colorant, pigment or dyes that can come from animal, plant, mineral and chemical sources,

24
00:01:58,341 --> 00:02:04,360
and the vehicle, usually a water or oil-based substance which can control viscosity and

25
00:02:04,361 --> 00:02:08,360
affect the dry rate.

26
00:02:08,361 --> 00:02:13,360
Ink may also contain other ingredients used to modify its properties, depending on the

27
00:02:13,361 --> 00:02:14,632
intended use.

28
00:02:14,633 --> 00:02:22,360
Sun Chemical, the world's largest ink manufacturer, makes inks for a variety of

29
00:02:22,361 --> 00:02:28,040
purposes, specifically developing formulations and colors unique to their customers' needs.

30
00:02:30,040 --> 00:02:34,040
One of their most important products is publication gravure ink,

31
00:02:35,360 --> 00:02:41,920
a low-viscosity quick-drying ink most commonly used to reproduce text and pictures onto a paper

32
00:02:41,921 --> 00:02:49,260
substrate.

33
00:02:49,261 --> 00:02:55,280
Sun Chemicals Publication Gravure Ink Plant in Kankakee, Illinois, turns out nearly 150

34
00:02:55,281 --> 00:03:01,080
million pounds of product every year.

35
00:03:01,081 --> 00:03:08,920
The raw materials, pigments, solvents and varnishes, arrive via railcar.

36
00:03:08,921 --> 00:03:15,920
The solvents and varnishes travel by pipe into the facility.

37
00:03:15,921 --> 00:03:21,540
A forklift bearing a 2,000-pound bag of carbon black pigment powder rolls in to commence

38
00:03:21,541 --> 00:03:25,160
the ink mixing operation.

39
00:03:25,161 --> 00:03:30,060
We raise the bag up into a hopper where the material is added and then vacuum conveyed

40
00:03:30,061 --> 00:03:32,660
into another room where we do our premixing.

41
00:03:32,661 --> 00:03:38,420
In that process, the carbon black is it combined with varnishes and solvents under a very high

42
00:03:38,421 --> 00:03:43,380
speed high agitation blade and then from that point it moves on to our media mill processing

43
00:03:46,580 --> 00:03:50,580
the pigment and solvent mixture is pumped into a chamber through the bottom of the media mill

44
00:03:53,780 --> 00:03:57,540
inside the ink components pass over a series of metal discs

45
00:03:58,500 --> 00:04:04,180
and are broken down by 2 000 pounds of circulating 2 millimeter diameter ball bearings

46
00:04:04,181 --> 00:04:11,980
This causes the pigment particles to be ground, dispersed, and surrounded by the solvent.

47
00:04:11,981 --> 00:04:18,700
Those ball bearings actually act to reduce the particle size of the pigment, which then

48
00:04:18,701 --> 00:04:21,616
enhances the color strength of the material.

49
00:04:21,617 --> 00:04:24,400
The ink exits through the top of the media

50
00:04:24,401 --> 00:04:29,200
to be weighed, blended, quality checked, and filtered.

51
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:32,980
But before a single drop goes out,

52
00:04:33,780 --> 00:04:37,180
each batch is tested to make sure the color meets the necessary standards.

53
00:04:38,520 --> 00:04:41,020
Back in the Stone Age when I started doing this,

54
00:04:41,460 --> 00:04:43,520
we used to evaluate color visually.

55
00:04:43,760 --> 00:04:46,760
So you would describe it as strong or weak or different shape.

56
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:50,860
But now we have wonderful technology using spectrophotometers,

57
00:04:50,861 --> 00:04:55,440
which allows you to get an actual numerical measurement of color.

58
00:04:56,740 --> 00:04:59,640
The spectrophotometer is applied to the test area

59
00:04:59,641 --> 00:05:02,640
to evaluate color intensity and light absorption.

60
00:05:05,080 --> 00:05:07,100
The results are fed into a computer.

61
00:05:07,780 --> 00:05:10,620
It will determine if the color falls within the acceptable range.

62
00:05:12,880 --> 00:05:14,580
Once given the stamp of approval,

63
00:05:15,780 --> 00:05:18,920
the ink travels by tanker truck to the printing press facility.

64
00:05:20,860 --> 00:05:30,040
Because of Gravure Inc's low viscosity and quick dry properties, the Gravure printing

65
00:05:30,041 --> 00:05:39,180
process is ideal for long run magazines, catalogs, and newspaper inserts.

66
00:05:39,181 --> 00:05:46,660
So some of those goodies that end up in your mailbox start here.

67
00:05:46,661 --> 00:05:52,800
The huge Rotogravir printing press at Quebecore World can use up to 4,000 gallons of ink per day

68
00:05:52,801 --> 00:05:57,820
and can produce nearly 70,000 multi-page products per hour.

69
00:06:01,840 --> 00:06:04,060
This is where ink meets paper.

70
00:06:06,560 --> 00:06:13,080
When the web comes through a Rotogravir press, it actually touches the cylinder which has the engraving.

71
00:06:13,081 --> 00:06:19,640
it's like a static which pulls that ink right up out of the cells and allows it to adhere to the paper.

72
00:06:20,860 --> 00:06:22,760
Colors are applied lightest to darkest,

73
00:06:23,320 --> 00:06:27,140
and each layer is forced dry before moving on to the next color unit.

74
00:06:30,640 --> 00:06:33,300
All the colors that make up the pictures and text

75
00:06:33,301 --> 00:06:37,200
are a carefully controlled amalgamation of the four base process colors.

76
00:06:40,540 --> 00:06:42,660
When applied, the inks are translucent.

77
00:06:43,080 --> 00:06:46,760
meaning one color can be seen through another color

78
00:06:46,761 --> 00:06:52,080
and when over printed and combined in a variety of different proportions

79
00:06:52,081 --> 00:06:57,080
they can produce a wide range of colors

80
00:06:57,081 --> 00:07:01,520
tints or screens of yellow, magenta, cyan

81
00:07:01,521 --> 00:07:05,840
or black inks are printed in patterns of tiny dots

82
00:07:05,841 --> 00:07:09,080
the individual color layers overlap

83
00:07:09,081 --> 00:07:12,080
appearing as a percentage of a solid color

84
00:07:12,081 --> 00:07:16,940
When unmagnified, this gives the illusion of continuous tones.

85
00:07:18,940 --> 00:07:27,260
But some printing jobs, like labels and company logos, might require a consistent color precisely matched to the customer's specifications.

86
00:07:29,940 --> 00:07:38,180
For this purpose, ink manufacturers can create pre-mixed spot colors rather than using the four process color combinations.

87
00:07:38,181 --> 00:07:39,088
At the St.

88
00:07:39,089 --> 00:07:53,089
Charles facility, we manufacture a variety of different ink chemistries, ranging from screen printing ink to sheet-fed offset, plastic cup, and metal decorating.

89
00:07:54,520 --> 00:07:57,080
Our facility manufactures 9 million pounds a year.

90
00:07:58,120 --> 00:08:02,480
We manufacture every kind of color you could possibly imagine.

91
00:08:02,481 --> 00:08:07,460
Basically, a customer will request a certain color match for a certain job,

92
00:08:07,580 --> 00:08:11,500
and we can match that color, any particular color a customer wants.

93
00:08:12,380 --> 00:08:13,560
Sounds pretty simple.

94
00:08:14,120 --> 00:08:17,860
But the ink industry lives and dies by its ability to give the customer

95
00:08:17,861 --> 00:08:20,300
just the right color and chemistry for the job.

96
00:08:23,260 --> 00:08:26,020
After a spot color has been deemed spot on,

97
00:08:26,980 --> 00:08:28,900
color formulas are sent to manufacturing.

98
00:08:28,901 --> 00:08:33,840
The pigment component is a thick paste known as flush,

99
00:08:34,440 --> 00:08:39,460
which is a pigment powder combined with a non-volatile dispersant, such as water.

100
00:08:40,500 --> 00:08:45,240
Following the color recipe, a predetermined number of flush containers is added to the vehicle.

101
00:08:46,740 --> 00:08:48,840
In this case, a soy oil base.

102
00:08:50,120 --> 00:08:55,120
What we've just completed doing is adding the rubine flush to this particular blending ink.

103
00:08:55,121 --> 00:09:00,920
The operator will get as close as they can by adding the correct number of containers

104
00:09:00,921 --> 00:09:06,700
and dig out the material to get directly to the exact weight that's needed for the batch.

105
00:09:08,940 --> 00:09:14,600
Once the pigment flush and vehicle meet, they move to the mixer to get better acquainted.

106
00:09:15,900 --> 00:09:18,300
This mixer is called the coaxial mixer.

107
00:09:18,301 --> 00:09:25,460
The large bowtie blade is used to move the bulk of the fluid around the batch to keep it mixing uniformly.

108
00:09:26,120 --> 00:09:30,580
And the high-speed sawtooth blade chops up the flush into the vehicle.

109
00:09:30,760 --> 00:09:38,340
So after probably about an hour's worth of mixing time, that flush will be broken down into the vehicle and it'll be a nice, smooth, homogeneous mix.

110
00:09:39,900 --> 00:09:43,960
The ink is conveyed through rollers that further break down the pigment particles.

111
00:09:43,961 --> 00:09:52,200
Then, it's packaged and shipped to customers, like Berry Plastics.

112
00:09:52,201 --> 00:09:59,440
In this facility, we manufacture food containers, and we also print all the containers that

113
00:09:59,441 --> 00:10:01,520
we manufacture.

114
00:10:01,521 --> 00:10:09,080
All our inks need to be scratch-resistant because cups are stacked inside each other.

115
00:10:09,081 --> 00:10:14,500
Instead of using a drying process, these spot color inks are cured with ultraviolet light

116
00:10:14,501 --> 00:10:22,460
onto a non-porous plastic surface at a rate of 10 cuffs per second.

117
00:10:22,461 --> 00:10:29,000
Once the containers are printed and stacked, they go into a box labeled with… what else?

118
00:10:29,001 --> 00:10:32,500
More ink.

119
00:10:32,501 --> 00:10:36,560
Ink can change the look of any shaped surface.

120
00:10:36,561 --> 00:10:41,980
But what if you're looking to print on a surface whose shape will change?

121
00:10:41,981 --> 00:10:47,880
Enter the aptly named flexographic inks, commonly used to print items like food packaging,

122
00:10:47,881 --> 00:10:58,320
wallpaper, plastic bags, and today, yellow smiley face balloons.

123
00:10:58,321 --> 00:11:05,320
This yellow ink recipe contains additives, like wax, that make it flexible and scuff resistant.

124
00:11:09,820 --> 00:11:13,320
Then it's out of the kitchen and into the press.

125
00:11:15,320 --> 00:11:22,320
The ink meets the metalized nylon substrate and is conveyed to an electric compressed air dryer.

126
00:11:23,320 --> 00:11:25,320
Just how fast is all this moving?

127
00:11:25,321 --> 00:11:29,740
The maximum foot per minute that the machine can do is 1,500 feet per minute.

128
00:11:30,080 --> 00:11:36,020
And this type of material will generally run 20,000 foot rolls, which would yield around 14,000 balloons.

129
00:11:39,080 --> 00:11:42,840
And those balloons will make for a lot of smiles in a lot of photos.

130
00:11:45,040 --> 00:11:47,420
Photos you can print without leaving your house.

131
00:11:50,420 --> 00:11:53,020
Thanks to some other very high-tech inks.

132
00:11:53,021 --> 00:11:57,880
descendants of a substance that's nearly 5,000 years old.

133
00:12:01,500 --> 00:12:06,140
Sun Chemical produces over 2 billion pounds of ink worldwide each year,

134
00:12:06,880 --> 00:12:11,760
equivalent to the volume of 357 Olympic-sized swimming pools.

135
00:12:13,060 --> 00:12:17,860
Ink will return on Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

136
00:12:17,861 --> 00:12:24,860
We now return to ink on Modern Marvels.

137
00:12:27,860 --> 00:12:31,860
With just an inkjet printer and the obligatory cartridges,

138
00:12:32,860 --> 00:12:36,860
vivid high-resolution photos appear at the touch of a button.

139
00:12:41,860 --> 00:12:44,860
Inside these cartridges are the specially formulated inks

140
00:12:44,861 --> 00:12:46,860
that make the images come alive.

141
00:12:47,860 --> 00:12:50,700
and help them stay looking that way.

142
00:12:55,580 --> 00:12:59,200
Hewlett-Packard scientists make inkjet ink from water,

143
00:13:00,280 --> 00:13:01,080
dyes or pigments,

144
00:13:01,520 --> 00:13:04,180
and various additives that control specific properties.

145
00:13:06,260 --> 00:13:11,140
We need to have ingredients that help prevent the ink from drying out

146
00:13:11,141 --> 00:13:14,700
on the surface of the printhead, okay,

147
00:13:14,701 --> 00:13:16,180
because it's in contact with air.

148
00:13:16,181 --> 00:13:18,460
So we have to add what we call humectants.

149
00:13:18,720 --> 00:13:22,480
We have to add things which help keep the ink stable,

150
00:13:22,660 --> 00:13:26,680
help keep the colorant correctly dissolved in the ink.

151
00:13:26,940 --> 00:13:32,140
We have ingredients that help maintain the correct pH or acidity of the ink.

152
00:13:32,600 --> 00:13:37,720
We have to add ingredients that help bind the colorant to the surface of the paper.

153
00:13:39,840 --> 00:13:44,040
Years of research are spent to ensure that the ink is applied to the surface properly.

154
00:13:44,041 --> 00:13:48,900
And once it's applied, the ink will stand the test of time.

155
00:13:50,620 --> 00:13:55,400
We have a print head, an inkjet print head in a pocket here.

156
00:13:55,800 --> 00:14:01,180
We have a camera below the pen that's watching the ink as it emerges from the nozzle plate.

157
00:14:01,840 --> 00:14:07,460
We have a camera from the side watching the drops as they fly out of the print head.

158
00:14:07,940 --> 00:14:11,540
We're monitoring both cameras here with a computer and a monitor.

159
00:14:11,541 --> 00:14:18,240
and we're looking for good drop ejection efficiency, good directionality and consistency from the print head.

160
00:14:18,680 --> 00:14:24,340
What will happen occasionally if you have a bad ink or a print head that is in poor condition

161
00:14:24,341 --> 00:14:28,600
is that you'll see a lot of ink collecting here on the nozzle plate.

162
00:14:29,420 --> 00:14:33,540
And we don't like that because then that puddle is going to interact with the drop

163
00:14:33,541 --> 00:14:36,000
causing poor print quality on your document.

164
00:14:38,140 --> 00:14:41,440
A strobe light makes the drops appear frozen in time.

165
00:14:41,540 --> 00:14:45,540
to give the engineer a better look at the ink's ejection efficiency.

166
00:14:50,240 --> 00:14:53,140
Light has always been ink's greatest nemesis.

167
00:14:54,100 --> 00:14:58,540
The energy contained in light can trigger a chemical reaction in the colorant molecules,

168
00:14:59,380 --> 00:15:03,980
causing them to react with oxygen in the air and break down, losing their color.

169
00:15:05,080 --> 00:15:06,840
So there's a test for that, too.

170
00:15:06,841 --> 00:15:13,840
The trick to correctly accelerating light fade is to shine very bright lights on the samples,

171
00:15:13,841 --> 00:15:16,840
but do it in a way where you're controlling the temperature and humidity.

172
00:15:16,841 --> 00:15:20,840
And the way to do that is to build or buy something like one of these units.

173
00:15:20,841 --> 00:15:26,840
What this device allows us to do is shine extremely bright lights onto the samples

174
00:15:26,841 --> 00:15:30,840
while still controlling the temperature and humidity to be what it would be in your home.

175
00:15:30,841 --> 00:15:34,840
To give you an idea of the brightness, they're about 90,000 lux,

176
00:15:34,841 --> 00:15:40,680
and compared to a typical room, this is a couple of hundred times brighter than the typical room.

177
00:15:41,120 --> 00:15:44,460
If a photograph can last, say, a month or two in a year,

178
00:15:44,860 --> 00:15:49,280
that translates into many years, decades, in the real world.

179
00:15:51,880 --> 00:15:55,580
Only the highest scoring ink formulas make it into the HP cartridges.

180
00:15:58,720 --> 00:16:01,460
So what exactly happens when you click print?

181
00:16:01,461 --> 00:16:07,360
An integrated circuit routes signals to the correct ink nozzle with amazing precision.

182
00:16:09,940 --> 00:16:16,580
Each ink drop is propelled by a superheated vapor explosion that lasts just over 2 microseconds,

183
00:16:18,200 --> 00:16:22,700
delivering up to 36,000 drops per second from each of the printhead's nozzles.

184
00:16:24,700 --> 00:16:28,240
As many as 32 ink drops can make up a single color dot.

185
00:16:31,460 --> 00:16:37,460
A standard 4x6 inkjet photograph can contain up to 35 million of these ink dots.

186
00:16:41,540 --> 00:16:46,660
All of this amazing ink technology evolved from one of man's simplest needs.

187
00:16:47,800 --> 00:16:54,460
Ink enables us to record history, record our knowledge, and pass it on from one generation to another.

188
00:16:54,880 --> 00:17:00,340
So if you look at a historical context, the history of ink is the history of civilization.

189
00:17:00,341 --> 00:17:05,280
and if you put it up against all the great inventions of early civilization,

190
00:17:05,281 --> 00:17:09,420
it ranks right up there with the wheel and agriculture.

191
00:17:10,920 --> 00:17:14,360
The earliest form of ink, commonly known as India ink,

192
00:17:15,100 --> 00:17:19,160
was actually created by the Chinese around 2500 B.C.

193
00:17:19,820 --> 00:17:22,960
It was a mixture of soot from pine smoke, lamp oil,

194
00:17:23,480 --> 00:17:26,980
gelatin from animal skins, and musk to hide the bad smell.

195
00:17:30,340 --> 00:17:36,240
The ancient Egyptians added colors derived from crushed berries and minerals to the inks

196
00:17:36,241 --> 00:17:40,600
they used.

197
00:17:40,601 --> 00:17:46,100
But some of the best ink producers aren't human at all.

198
00:17:46,101 --> 00:17:52,500
Octopus, squid and cuttlefish squirt a brown liquid when threatened.

199
00:17:52,501 --> 00:17:58,140
Contained in the ink sack, the cloudy substance known as sepia acts as a smokescreen to deflect

200
00:17:58,141 --> 00:18:02,540
predators.

201
00:18:02,541 --> 00:18:08,120
Ancient Romans used the cephalopods' ink sacks to make ink.

202
00:18:08,121 --> 00:18:16,520
Today the ink is used primarily to color and flavor food.

203
00:18:16,521 --> 00:18:22,500
In ancient cultures, mainly the learned and privileged had access to ink.

204
00:18:22,501 --> 00:18:28,880
No one else really needed it or knew what to do with it.

205
00:18:28,881 --> 00:18:32,600
One of the first known uses for ink was to keep farm accounts.

206
00:18:32,601 --> 00:18:38,640
So there was no guessing as to who owned what and who was owed what.

207
00:18:38,641 --> 00:18:45,380
As keeping records became more and more important, we looked for ways to make more permanent inks.

208
00:18:45,381 --> 00:18:50,180
Iron gall ink was used throughout the Middle Ages up until the 19th century.

209
00:18:50,181 --> 00:18:58,020
It was made from a combination of crushed gall nuts, an iron or copper salt, and a binding

210
00:18:58,021 --> 00:19:04,200
medium such as gum arabic.

211
00:19:04,201 --> 00:19:10,380
These are gall nuts which are actually bulbous growths that form on trees.

212
00:19:10,381 --> 00:19:17,200
These are full of tannic and gallic acid and to make the ink they were crushed and infused

213
00:19:17,201 --> 00:19:18,420
in water.

214
00:19:18,421 --> 00:19:25,180
The extract from the gall nuts and the iron salt would combine together to make a complex

215
00:19:25,181 --> 00:19:28,020
called an iron gallotannate complex.

216
00:19:28,460 --> 00:19:36,080
The iron gallotannate complex oxidizes very quickly and forms a sort of brown-black color.

217
00:19:37,620 --> 00:19:40,560
Da Vinci wrote his notes with iron gall ink.

218
00:19:42,860 --> 00:19:44,120
Bach composed with it.

219
00:19:46,100 --> 00:19:47,900
Rembrandt and Van Gogh drew with it.

220
00:19:48,420 --> 00:19:55,420
The medieval and Renaissance masters liked the ink's rich dark brown color and durability.

221
00:19:55,421 --> 00:20:03,420
When exposed to oxygen, the ferric ions in the ink react with the gallotannic acid

222
00:20:03,421 --> 00:20:09,420
to form a strong, non-water-soluble chemical bond with the proteins in the parchment.

223
00:20:09,421 --> 00:20:10,530
parchment.

224
00:20:10,531 --> 00:20:19,080
Many very famous documents, such as the Dead Sea Scrolls and later documents,

225
00:20:19,081 --> 00:20:24,040
for example, the Declaration, were written with iron gall ink.

226
00:20:24,041 --> 00:20:26,520
It is very resistant to fading,

227
00:20:26,521 --> 00:20:32,280
and because of the chemistry of iron gall ink, it can't be easily removed.

228
00:20:32,281 --> 00:20:38,280
Throughout the Middle Ages, in many parts of the world,

229
00:20:38,281 --> 00:20:43,320
scribes and artists added ornate ink decorations to the text of selected

230
00:20:43,321 --> 00:20:49,200
manuscripts to indicate that these were items of true significance. It was called

231
00:20:49,201 --> 00:20:56,460
illumination. Blurring the line between ink and paint, the adornment often came

232
00:20:56,461 --> 00:21:01,800
in the form of decorated letters and pictures done in bright colors with gold

233
00:21:01,801 --> 00:21:06,800
and silver accents.

234
00:21:06,801 --> 00:21:12,480
The colored substances were handmade in small quantities. Artisans created

235
00:21:12,481 --> 00:21:19,680
pigments by extracting and pulverizing materials found in nature. Reds came from

236
00:21:19,681 --> 00:21:24,900
the dried bodies of Kermes insects, roots of the matter plant or iron oxide rich

237
00:21:24,901 --> 00:21:27,300
earth.

238
00:21:27,301 --> 00:21:31,560
Yellows often came from saffron or turmeric.

239
00:21:31,561 --> 00:21:38,360
Green inks got their color from buckthorn berries or copper acetate known as verdigris.

240
00:21:38,361 --> 00:21:45,280
The pigments were bound with egg whites or gum arabic to make the final product.

241
00:21:45,281 --> 00:21:48,640
Both religious and secular manuscripts were illuminated.

242
00:21:48,641 --> 00:21:52,840
Because of this beautification, the books were treasured by their owners and eventually

243
00:21:52,841 --> 00:22:00,700
by collectors, thus preserving the messages held in the iron gall ink text.

244
00:22:00,701 --> 00:22:07,920
Iron gall was the ink of choice in many parts of the world for over 700 years.

245
00:22:07,921 --> 00:22:13,900
By the mid-1800s, the fountain pen became more common than the quill.

246
00:22:13,901 --> 00:22:18,900
Writers found that the iron gall ink clogged the thin carrier channel, and the salt content

247
00:22:18,901 --> 00:22:20,340
corroded the pen's metal tip.

248
00:22:22,920 --> 00:22:26,960
New watery inks containing non-corrosive synthetic dyes

249
00:22:26,961 --> 00:22:28,900
were specially developed for fountain pens.

250
00:22:32,980 --> 00:22:37,080
Patented in 1938, the ballpoint pen worked well

251
00:22:37,081 --> 00:22:38,740
with viscous, quicker drying ink.

252
00:22:40,460 --> 00:22:43,040
It made writing a faster and neater endeavor.

253
00:22:44,340 --> 00:22:47,860
If you could ignore the occasional clumps caused by excess ink

254
00:22:47,861 --> 00:22:49,480
collecting on the lip around the ball.

255
00:22:53,320 --> 00:22:56,860
Ballpoint pens ultimately became the inexpensive, dominant option

256
00:22:56,861 --> 00:22:58,260
for putting ink on paper.

257
00:23:01,980 --> 00:23:06,100
Over the years, ink has gone from precious to prevalent

258
00:23:06,101 --> 00:23:09,820
and made the leap from handmade small batches

259
00:23:09,821 --> 00:23:14,680
to industrial-sized, computer-controlled, commercial commodity

260
00:23:14,681 --> 00:23:22,380
And a large portion of that commodity is still used to spread news to the masses.

