264
00:23:44,680 --> 00:23:51,680
Fast and efficient reproduction of the written word is where ink really took hold in the modern world.

265
00:23:51,681 --> 00:24:02,680
The invention of the movable type printing press in the 15th century helped spread religion and shape perception.

266
00:24:02,681 --> 00:24:11,680
Ink was used to create writing and drawings to convey information from one person to another or from one group of people to another people.

267
00:24:11,681 --> 00:24:12,982
to other people.

268
00:24:12,983 --> 00:24:18,680
Today, ink is as vital as ever when it comes to spreading information.

269
00:24:18,681 --> 00:24:25,680
About 15% of all ink produced in the U.S. is used in the newspaper industry.

270
00:24:25,681 --> 00:24:30,680
Here at the Milwaukee Journal Sentinel, our daily circulation, that is, would be Monday through Friday.

271
00:24:30,681 --> 00:24:37,680
Our circulation would be about 240,000. Sunday, we're just over 400,000 copies.

272
00:24:37,681 --> 00:24:43,140
And at the root of this, the original information superhighway is ink.

273
00:24:44,240 --> 00:24:50,540
There are six ink tanks in our ink room, two black ink tanks with us holding 7,700 gallons each,

274
00:24:50,720 --> 00:24:52,820
one cyan, one magenta, and one yellow.

275
00:24:53,100 --> 00:24:55,380
Each one of those holds about 3,000 gallons of ink.

276
00:24:58,340 --> 00:25:03,640
This may resemble the web-fed, four-color, oven-dried gravure ink and printing process,

277
00:25:04,640 --> 00:25:08,660
But the ink used for newspapers is a thick, oil-based paste

278
00:25:08,661 --> 00:25:12,080
that dries slowly by absorption into the paper fibers.

279
00:25:15,660 --> 00:25:19,840
What distinguishes newspaper printing is really there's no dryers in the process.

280
00:25:20,660 --> 00:25:25,060
So typically after printing, the ink bleeps down into the fibers of the sheet,

281
00:25:25,500 --> 00:25:28,340
and that usually typically takes between 3 to 12 hours.

282
00:25:29,880 --> 00:25:32,780
By then, the paper is already sitting on your doorstep.

283
00:25:33,640 --> 00:25:38,340
And if the ink isn't fully dry, some could end up on your fingers.

284
00:25:42,480 --> 00:25:48,580
The printing process wasn't always so quick, and the early printing ink didn't come in giant tanks.

285
00:25:49,960 --> 00:25:57,240
In the mid-15th century, Johann Gutenberg specially formulated small batches of ink for his little invention.

286
00:25:59,060 --> 00:26:01,200
The movable type printing press.

287
00:26:03,640 --> 00:26:09,380
Water-based ink was good for writing, but it didn't adhere well to the metal pieces used in the press.

288
00:26:10,980 --> 00:26:16,200
Gutenberg used lamp soot for color and replaced the water vehicle with linseed oil

289
00:26:16,201 --> 00:26:20,060
because it distributed more evenly and stuck to the type.

290
00:26:23,040 --> 00:26:27,560
This formula was the precursor to the petroleum-based printing inks developed since.

291
00:26:27,561 --> 00:26:35,820
Today, with recent endeavors to be environmentally responsible,

292
00:26:37,020 --> 00:26:41,480
most daily news publications have shifted to using soy-based color inks.

293
00:26:43,300 --> 00:26:48,220
Soy ink is made from soybean oil, which is made by crushing the beans in the process.

294
00:26:48,660 --> 00:26:53,600
It's a renewable product with low dependency on petroleum oils, which is diminishing.

295
00:26:53,601 --> 00:27:00,600
Also, the soil oil gives us a lower VOC, volatile organic component, which means less emissions,

296
00:27:00,601 --> 00:27:03,600
less air pollution produced.

297
00:27:03,601 --> 00:27:08,600
And it reduces the rub off on your fingers.

298
00:27:08,601 --> 00:27:14,600
Aside from automation innovations and some eco-friendly materials, newspapers haven't

299
00:27:14,601 --> 00:27:16,600
changed that much.

300
00:27:16,601 --> 00:27:22,600
It's always been some kind of ink on some kind of paper.

301
00:27:22,601 --> 00:27:29,760
But today, IKOS of Franklin, Massachusetts, is working with carbon nanotubing to take

302
00:27:29,761 --> 00:27:32,980
paper out of the equation.

303
00:27:32,981 --> 00:27:37,900
This revolutionary substance is busting the conventional definition of ink by enabling

304
00:27:37,901 --> 00:27:48,540
portable, battery-powered, flexible, real-time newspaper screens with movable print.

305
00:27:48,541 --> 00:27:52,820
The success of this technology can be based on the unique properties of carbon nanotubes.

306
00:27:54,260 --> 00:27:56,340
Each of these intersections is a carbon atom,

307
00:27:56,920 --> 00:28:00,480
and it forms these hexagons that look just like chicken wire.

308
00:28:01,140 --> 00:28:03,020
That chicken wire is wrapped into a tube.

309
00:28:04,380 --> 00:28:06,560
What makes these tubes interesting to scientists

310
00:28:06,561 --> 00:28:11,140
is that, like other forms of carbon, nanotubes conduct electricity.

311
00:28:14,860 --> 00:28:16,980
But unlike any other type of carbon,

312
00:28:16,981 --> 00:28:20,540
they form with an enormous width-to-length aspect ratio,

313
00:28:21,260 --> 00:28:25,300
roughly a thousand times longer than they are wide.

314
00:28:26,600 --> 00:28:29,600
To most, this may seem like microscopic minutiae,

315
00:28:29,920 --> 00:28:33,560
but it will change the very way we look at ink.

316
00:28:35,820 --> 00:28:39,780
When a thin coating of the ink is applied to a transparent plastic layer,

317
00:28:40,760 --> 00:28:42,280
the liquid evaporates,

318
00:28:42,820 --> 00:28:45,780
and you're left with conductive carbon nanotube wires.

319
00:28:46,980 --> 00:28:52,980
These wires form an interwoven network where electricity can pass from tube to tube.

320
00:28:53,980 --> 00:29:01,980
Two of these plastic layers sandwich pixels, made up of tiny electrically charged dots that are either white or colored.

321
00:29:02,980 --> 00:29:07,980
An electrical charge is supplied selectively to attract either the white or colored dots,

322
00:29:08,980 --> 00:29:13,980
creating the appearance of pictures, text, and even moving images.

323
00:29:13,981 --> 00:29:24,740
carbon nanotube coatings can also be extremely abrasion resistant and can be bent more than 25,000 times before failing

324
00:29:24,741 --> 00:29:34,260
transparent conductive and durable carbon nanotube ink is perfect for a new breed of electronic products

325
00:29:34,261 --> 00:29:41,840
it's a long way from the ink produced in China 5,000 years ago but in some ways it's remarkably similar

326
00:29:41,841 --> 00:29:44,940
and it's still doing the same thing of conveying information.

327
00:29:45,800 --> 00:29:48,960
But what if that information is deliberately concealed?

328
00:29:49,880 --> 00:29:54,320
With a little help from science, ink can reveal what was once hidden.

